---
name: reference-windows-gate-runs
description: "How to run the Windows quality gates and the real-GPU suite from WSL for this repo (cmd.exe + .bat in the user's Temp dir)"
metadata:
  node_type: memory
  type: reference
  originSessionId: a4687e22-62cc-49f2-88b8-2ac41e53e24d
  modified: 2026-10-22T22:06:46.509Z
---

Windows gates cannot run from WSL's toolchain. They run through `cmd.exe` on the Windows host, which is the same tree at `D:\dev\haiku_game_engine`.

- Write a `.bat` that calls `vcvars64.bat` (VS 18 Community), `cd /d D:\dev\haiku_game_engine`, then runs each gate and prints `name=%ERRORLEVEL%` on its own line with no redirect. Copy it to `/mnt/c/Users/bucha/AppData/Local/Temp/` and launch from WSL as `cmd.exe /c "C:\\Users\\bucha\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\<name>.bat" > result.txt 2>&1`, then read the codes from `result.txt`.
- The `.bat` needs CRLF line endings: after writing it from WSL, run `sed -i 's/$/\r/' <file>`.
- Do not write exit codes with `echo name=%ERRORLEVEL%>> "%RES%"`. On 2026-10-22 that line printed a blank code and wrote no file, even with CRLF. The stdout capture above worked, and it is the only method checked so far.
- On 2026-10-23 (US-017) the `echo step=%ERRORLEVEL%` lines did not reach the stdout capture, which held only `[exited with code 0]`. Use `set RC=%ERRORLEVEL%` then `(echo rc=%RC%)> %TEMP%\rc.txt` on its own line, and read the per-step logs from Temp.
- Run one gate batch at a time. A second `cmd.exe` during a run makes both fail on file locks.
- The Windows host has a real NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080, so `engine_tests --suite gpu --backend dx12` runs there and is a valid GPU report. WSL and software adapters are not.
- Check the logs, not only exit codes: a build log can be short when the build directory is already warm.
- On 2026-10-23 (US-033) a relaunch while the first run was still going mixed both runs in one `*_rc.txt`, and the GPU suites ran a stale `engine_tests.exe`. Before relaunching, wait until `pgrep -f <bat name>` finds nothing, and delete the result file then. A failed build does not stop the batch, so the test steps after it run an old binary: check the build log before you trust `unit=0` or `gpu=0`.
----
---
name: feedback-windows-gate-overlap
description: "Before launching a Windows gate or GPU batch from WSL, confirm no earlier cmd.exe batch is still running; a stale batch keeps running its GPU steps after a relaunch"
metadata:
  node_type: memory
  type: feedback
  originSessionId: 30fb1424-41dc-4e24-9498-83de33d6f9dd
  modified: 2026-10-23T19:50:41.209Z
---

On 2026-10-23 (US-038) a relaunch of the Windows gate batch started while the first batch was still in its GPU step. Two batches shared
the Temp result files, and the first one kept running a stale `engine_tests.exe`. The fix was to terminate the matching processes and relaunch.

**Why:** the first batch had reported `build=2` (a compile error) and then went on to the GPU steps with the old binary, so its numbers
looked like results of the new code.

**How to apply:**
- Before each launch, check for the batch by its own name in the process list (`ps -eo pid,args | grep <name>.bat`). Use a name that
  does not also appear in the command you run to check.
- To stop leftovers, use PowerShell `Get-CimInstance Win32_Process` filtered by command line, then `Invoke-CimMethod ... Terminate`. The
  filter string also matches the PowerShell process itself, so use a pattern that the check command does not contain.
- A build failure does not stop the batch. Read the build log before trusting `unit=` or `gpu_*=` lines.
- Keep one background waiter per batch, and make it wait on the batch's done file with a bounded timeout. Repeated Monitors on the same
  file re-deliver the same result many times.
- 2026-10-23 (US-043) it happened again: I relaunched after the done file was written, but the batch's GPU steps were still running, so
  the link failed with LNK1168 (`engine_tests.exe` locked) and the rc file mixed both runs. Check the process list after the done file,
  not only the done file. `tasklist /FI` fails under WSL's `cmd.exe` quoting; run `cmd.exe /c tasklist` and grep, or run a `.ps1` file.
  Kill a stale batch with `taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F` (the `/T` takes its children), then relaunch.

